---
import Lesson from "../../layouts/Lesson.astro";
import Annotated from "../../components/Annotated.astro";
import Mixup from "../../components/Mixup.astro";
import Scenario from "../../components/Scenario.astro";
import type {
  CheckQuestion,
  Exhibit,
  Scenario as ScenarioData,
} from "../../data/learn/types";

// The example token follows the JWT profile for access tokens (RFC 9068), abbreviated,
// as the tokens in data/tokens.ts and data/verify.ts do; this lesson must not contradict them.
// 1794305400 is 10:10:00 UTC on November 10, 2026.
const claims = (scope = "calendar.read"): Exhibit => ({
  caption: "The token’s claims",
  lang: "json",
  text: `{
  "iss": "https://auth.example",
  "sub": "alice",
  "client_id": "calendar-assistant",
  "aud": "https://calendar.example",
  "scope": "${scope}",
  "exp": 1794305400
}`,
});
const at = (api: string, time: string, request: string): Exhibit => ({
  caption: "What the API compares them with",
  lang: "yaml",
  text: `this API:  ${api}
time now:  ${time}
request:   ${request}`,
});

const SKIP = {
  verdict: "skipped",
  note: "Not reached.",
} as const;

const checksScenario: ScenarioData = {
  id: "token-checks",
  legend: "What happens to the token",
  cast: [
    { label: "Agent", sub: "holds the token", role: "agent" },
    { label: "Calendar API", sub: "checks every token", role: "resource" },
  ],
  asks: "The API’s five checks, in order",
  questions: [
    "From an issuer I trust?",
    "Signature intact?",
    "Addressed to me?",
    "Still in date?",
    "Covers this request?",
  ],
  options: [
    {
      id: "asis",
      name: "Nothing: sent as issued",
      hops: ["a request, with the token"],
      exhibits: [
        claims(),
        at("calendar.example", "10:04", "GET /events?day=tomorrow"),
      ],
      findings: [
        {
          verdict: "yes",
          note: "`iss` is auth.example, which this API is set up to trust.",
        },
        {
          verdict: "yes",
          note: "It verifies with auth.example’s public key. Nothing has changed since it was signed.",
        },
        {
          verdict: "yes",
          note: "`aud` is calendar.example. That is this API.",
        },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`exp` is 10:10. It is 10:04." },
        {
          verdict: "yes",
          note: "The request reads events, and `scope` allows `calendar.read`.",
        },
      ],
      so: `All five pass, so the API believes the claims: these are Alice’s events, for the app she approved. **It did not have to ask anyone at the moment of the request, and it did not have to trust the agent.** The signature is the proof.`,
    },
    {
      id: "edited",
      name: "Someone edits it",
      hops: ["a request, with the token changed to allow writing"],
      exhibits: [
        claims("calendar.write"),
        at("calendar.example", "10:04", "DELETE /events/42"),
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`iss` still says auth.example." },
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "The claims no longer match the signature. Only auth.example’s private key could sign the new version.",
        },
        SKIP,
        SKIP,
        SKIP,
      ],
      so: `Rejected with \`401\`. **Anyone can read a token. Only the service that signs it can change one.** Nothing in a token that fails this check is believed, so the API stops here.`,
    },
    {
      id: "elsewhere",
      name: "It is sent to another API",
      cast: [
        { label: "Agent", sub: "holds the token", role: "agent" },
        { label: "Mail API", sub: "a different service", role: "resource" },
      ],
      hops: ["a request, with the calendar token"],
      exhibits: [claims(), at("mail.example", "10:04", "GET /messages")],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "The mail API trusts auth.example too." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "Unchanged since it was signed." },
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "`aud` is calendar.example. This is mail.example.",
        },
        SKIP,
        SKIP,
      ],
      so: `Rejected. A token is addressed to one API, and every API has to read the address. **If the mail API skipped this check, any service that was ever handed a token could turn around and use it somewhere else.**`,
    },
    {
      id: "late",
      name: "It is used an hour later",
      hops: ["a request, with the token, at 11:04"],
      exhibits: [
        claims(),
        at("calendar.example", "11:04", "GET /events?day=tomorrow"),
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`iss` is auth.example." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "Unchanged since it was signed." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`aud` is calendar.example." },
        { verdict: "no", note: "`exp` was 10:10. It is 11:04." },
        SKIP,
      ],
      so: `Rejected with \`401\`. Access tokens are short-lived on purpose, often a few minutes. The agent can get a fresh one without sending Alice through sign-in again; the next lesson shows how.`,
    },
    {
      id: "more",
      name: "The agent tries to delete an event",
      hops: ["a request to delete an event, with the token"],
      exhibits: [
        claims(),
        at("calendar.example", "10:04", "DELETE /events/42"),
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`iss` is auth.example." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "Unchanged since it was signed." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`aud` is calendar.example." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`exp` is 10:10. It is 10:04." },
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "Deleting needs `calendar.write`. Alice approved `calendar.read`.",
        },
      ],
      so: `Refused with \`403\`. The token is genuine, and it does not cover this. **It does not matter why the agent tried**: a bug, a bad instruction, or something it read on a web page. The limit holds.`,
    },
    {
      id: "copied",
      name: "Someone else sends a copy",
      cast: [
        { label: "Someone else", sub: "has a copy", role: "agent" },
        { label: "Calendar API", sub: "checks every token", role: "resource" },
      ],
      hops: ["a request, with the copied token"],
      exhibits: [
        claims(),
        at("calendar.example", "10:04", "GET /events?day=tomorrow"),
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`iss` is auth.example." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "Unchanged since it was signed." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`aud` is calendar.example." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`exp` is 10:10. It is 10:04." },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`scope` allows `calendar.read`." },
      ],
      so: `All five pass, and the API answers. **Nothing in this token says who is allowed to send it.** A token like this is called a *bearer* token: it works for whoever holds it, like cash. A short life limits how long a stolen copy is useful. The last lesson shows how to make a copy useless.`,
    },
  ],
};

const checks: CheckQuestion[] = [
  {
    id: "aud",
    question:
      "An API receives a token. The issuer is trusted, the signature is intact and it has not expired, but `aud` names a different API. What should it do?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "Accept it. Everything that matters checked out.",
        why: "The address matters as much as the signature. An API that accepts tokens meant for other services lets anyone who receives a token reuse it here.",
      },
      {
        text: "Reject it. The token was not issued for this API.",
        correct: true,
        why: "A token is addressed to one API. Checking `aud` is what stops a token issued for one service from being replayed at another.",
      },
      {
        text: "Accept it, but only for read requests.",
        why: "There is no partial credit. A token for a different audience says nothing about what its holder may do here.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "edit",
    question:
      "Before sending the token, the agent changes `calendar.read` to `calendar.write` in the claims. What happens?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "The API accepts it, because the token came from a trusted issuer.",
        why: "The issuer signed the original claims, not the edited ones. The signature no longer matches.",
      },
      {
        text: "The API rejects it, because the signature no longer matches the claims.",
        correct: true,
        why: "The signature covers every claim. Change one character and it fails, and only the issuer holds the key that could sign the new version.",
      },
      {
        text: "The authorization server notices and revokes the token.",
        why: "The authorization server is not involved at this point. The API detects the change itself, by checking the signature.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "short",
    question: "Why do access tokens usually last minutes and not months?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "Because the issuer’s signing key changes every few minutes.",
        why: "Signing keys are replaced far less often than that. The expiry is a choice the issuer makes for each token.",
      },
      {
        text: "Because a copied token works for whoever holds it, and a short life limits how long that is.",
        correct: true,
        why: "A bearer token cannot tell its rightful holder from a thief. Expiry is the simplest limit on what a stolen copy is worth.",
      },
      {
        text: "To make users sign in more often.",
        why: "The user is usually not asked again. With a refresh token the agent renews on its own, which the next lesson covers.",
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Lesson
  slug="tokens"
  lede="A few lines of text, signed by a service the API already trusts. The API checks the text. It never has to trust whoever is carrying it."
  checks={checks}
  keep={[
    "A token is a statement signed by an **authorization server**: who it is about, which app it was given to, which API it is for, what it allows, and until when.",
    "The API checks five things before believing any of it: issuer, signature, audience, expiry and scope.",
    "**Audience** is the check that gets forgotten. A token is addressed to one API.",
    "A valid token proves what was granted. It does not prove who is sending it: a plain token is a **bearer token**.",
  ]}
  names={[
    [
      "The service that checks who people are and issues tokens",
      "Authorization server",
    ],
    ["The token an API accepts", "Access token"],
    ["One line inside a token", "Claim"],
    ["Who signed it", "Issuer"],
    ["Who it is about", "Subject"],
    ["Which API it is for", "Audience"],
    ["What it allows", "Scope"],
    ["Three chunks of text joined by dots", "JWT"],
    ["A token that works for whoever holds it", "Bearer token"],
  ]}
>
  <h2>A key card, not a passport</h2>
  <p>
    A hotel key card is a fair picture of a token. The front desk checks who you are, once, and hands you a card. The
    card opens your room and the gym, in this hotel, until checkout. The door never asks for your passport. It checks
    the card.
  </p>
  <p>
    An <strong>access token</strong> is the card. The front desk is called an <strong>authorization server</strong>:
    the one service that checks who people are, asks what they agree to, and issues tokens. The door is the API.
  </p>
  <p>That is as far as the picture needs to go. What makes a token work is a signature.</p>

  <h2>What is written on it</h2>
  <p>
    Many access tokens are <strong>JWTs</strong> (said “jots”): three chunks of text joined by dots. You have
    probably seen one.
  </p>

  <div class="jwt wide" role="img" aria-label="A JWT in three parts: a header, the claims, and a signature over both.">
    <div>
      <code>eyJhbGciOiJFUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6ImF0K2p3dCJ9</code>
      <strong>Header</strong>
      <span>How it was signed</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <code>eyJpc3MiOiJodHRwczovL2F1dGguZXhhbXBsZSIsInN1YiI6ImFsaWNl…</code>
      <strong>Claims</strong>
      <span>The statement itself</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <code>q7Zl2mXk3vT9qLz0aB7r…</code>
      <strong>Signature</strong>
      <span>The issuer’s seal over the other two parts</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The first two chunks are not encrypted. They are JSON, encoded so that they survive a trip through an HTTP header.
    Decode the middle one and you get a handful of lines, each called a <strong>claim</strong>:
  </p>

  <Annotated
    caption="The claims of an example access token, abbreviated"
    head={['In the token', 'What it says', 'Which question']}
    rows={[
      {
        code: '"iss": "https://auth.example"',
        says: 'The **issuer**. The authorization server at auth.example signed this.',
        tag: 'Who said they could',
      },
      { code: '"sub": "alice"', says: 'The **subject**. It is about Alice.', tag: 'For whom' },
      {
        code: '"client_id": "calendar-assistant"',
        says: 'It was issued to this app.',
        tag: 'Who is acting',
      },
      {
        code: '"aud": "https://calendar.example"',
        says: 'The **audience**. It is meant for the calendar API and for nothing else.',
        tag: 'Who said they could',
      },
      {
        code: '"scope": "calendar.read"',
        says: 'The **scope**. It allows reading the calendar.',
        tag: 'Who said they could',
      },
      {
        code: '"exp": 1794305400',
        says: 'It stops working at 10:10, ten minutes after it was issued. The number is seconds since 1970.',
        tag: 'Who said they could',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <Mixup belief="A token is encrypted, so it is safe to put things in it and safe to leave it lying around.">
    <p>
      A JWT like this one is signed, not encrypted. Anyone who holds one can read every claim; paste one into any
      decoder and see. The signature stops a token being changed. It does nothing to stop it being read, or used.
    </p>
  </Mixup>

  <p>
    The third chunk is the signature. The authorization server computes it from the first two chunks with a private
    key that never leaves it, and publishes the matching public key. Anyone can check the signature. Only the
    authorization server can make one.
  </p>

  <h2>Try to break it</h2>
  <p>
    An API believes nothing in a token until it has checked it. There are five checks, and each button below does
    something to the token. Before you press one, guess which check will catch it. One of the six gets past all five.
  </p>

  <Scenario scenario={checksScenario} glance="All six at a glance" />

  <h2>When the API says no</h2>
  <p>The two refusals in the figure are worth telling apart, because they send you to different places.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>401</strong> means the API did not accept the token at all. One of the first four checks failed, or no
      token arrived. If sign-in has just succeeded and the first call still fails, a common cause is the audience:
      the token was issued for a different address from the one the API knows itself by. Check, too, that the token
      is being sent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>403</strong> means the token is good and does not cover this request. Retrying will not help. The agent
      needs a wider grant, and that means asking whoever can give one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a valid token does not prove</h2>
  <p>
    The last button is the uncomfortable one. A token records what was granted. It does not record who is allowed to
    carry it. A copy taken from a log file, a debugging session or an agent’s memory works exactly as well as the
    original, until it expires.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the trade a <strong>bearer token</strong> makes: simple to use, and only as safe as the places it passes
    through. It is why tokens are short-lived, why each is addressed to one API, and why it matters so much where an
    agent keeps them.
  </p>
  <Mixup belief="If the signature checks out, the token is good.">
    <p>
      A valid signature means the token is genuine. It does not mean the token is for this API, still in date, or
      wide enough for this request, and it says nothing about who is sending it. The signature is the second of five
      checks, not the last.
    </p>
  </Mixup>

  <p>
    One more thing you will run into. Not every token can be read. Some are random strings that mean nothing by
    themselves, and the API asks the authorization server what each one stands for. Most of the checks then happen
    there, and comparing the scope with the request stays with the API. Asking is also how an API learns that a
    token was withdrawn early: one that only checks the signature goes on accepting a withdrawn token until it
    expires.
  </p>
</Lesson>

<style>
  /* the three parts of a JWT, side by side with room, stacked without */
  .jwt {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 13rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 1.4rem 0 1.6rem;
  }
  .jwt > div {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 0.1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-top: 3px solid var(--fg);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .jwt code {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .jwt strong {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .jwt span {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
</style>
